      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the dough: combine the flour, eggs, a pinch of salt and 80ml warm water in a bowl. Mix to a firm dough, then knead for 8 minutes until smooth and elastic. Wrap and rest for 30 minutes at room temperature."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the filling: combine the lamb mince, grated onion, 2 of the crushed garlic cloves, cumin, black pepper and a generous pinch of salt. Mix well."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use the digital scales to divide the dough into 4 portions. On a lightly floured surface, use the rolling pin to roll each portion as thin as possible (1 to 2mm). Cut into 3cm squares."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place a very small amount of filling (about the size of a hazelnut) in the centre of each square. Pinch the four corners together firmly over the filling to make a sealed parcel. Press all the joins tightly so they do not open during cooking."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Stir the remaining 2 crushed garlic cloves into the yoghurt with a pinch of salt. Set aside at room temperature."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring the large saucepan of well-salted water to a rolling boil. Cook the manti in batches for 8 to 10 minutes until the dough is tender. Drain well."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Melt the butter in a small pan over a medium heat until it foams and turns lightly golden. Remove from the heat and stir in the smoked paprika."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the manti between bowls. Spoon the garlicky yoghurt generously over them. Pour the paprika butter over the top, scatter with parsley and serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Dumplings burst during boiling"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The joins must be pressed very firmly. Wet your fingertip slightly before sealing each join and pinch with real pressure. Also make sure the filling is not overfilled, as steam pressure will force the seams open."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Dough is tough"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Tough dough means not enough resting time or the dough was not kneaded sufficiently to develop the gluten. Let it rest a full 30 minutes. Roll as thinly as you can without tearing."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Yoghurt curdles when it hits the hot dumplings"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring the yoghurt to room temperature before serving. Cold yoghurt added to hot manti separates quickly. Spoon it on just before the butter goes over, not before."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Manti are tiny dumplings found across Central Asia and the Middle East. Turkish manti are smaller and boiled or baked. Uzbek manti are larger and steamed. The yoghurt sauce is essential. Brown butter and dried mint finish the dish. Make extra dumplings and freeze them before cooking."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
